00:01:05.019 --> 00:01:07.380
So I said we'd do a little
bit of level setting.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:09.990
So first, let's talk
about what Istio is just

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:13.437
for those people
that are new to this.

00:01:13.437 --> 00:01:16.020
And to talk about Istio, we have
to talk about a service mesh.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.450
So what is a service mesh?

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:21.100
Service meshes work
at the network layer.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:22.740
So you put them in.

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:25.380
They are transparent
to the application.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:28.740
That mean we don't care what
language the application is

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:34.920
written in, whether it's Java
or C# or Go or Node or whatever.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:36.420
It just doesn't
matter because we're

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.260
working at the network layer.

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:41.400
And the mesh provides some
level of functionality.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:45.680
Whether it's telemetry
or some network controls,

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:48.270
a service mesh provides
that kind of capability.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:51.140
And there are many
service meshes out there.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:52.700
Istio is probably
the most common one

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:53.700
that you've heard about.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:55.670
But there are other
service meshes as well.

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:01.740
So let's talk about
Istio specifically then.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:03.720
It is a service mesh.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:05.950
But we think it's
much more than that.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:08.699
It's an open services
platform because we've

00:02:08.699 --> 00:02:12.760
designed so many sensibility
points into Istio, again,

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:14.430
it works on containers and VM.

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:16.560
So we can bring in
the full environment.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.840
You can plug in different
monitoring solutions.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:22.180
You can plug in all kinds
of access control solutions.

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:25.530
So it really is a platform that
allows you to really control

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:29.070
your services, both what's
going on at the network level

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:33.830
and monitoring all
that information.

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:36.430
So what is the value
proposition of Istio then?

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:38.680
We break it down into
these three things,

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:40.900
uniform observability.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:42.430
Because we're at
the network layer,

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:44.830
we see all the network
traffic, and we know

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:47.120
what's going on on the network.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:49.480
And we can send all that
telemetry information

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:52.875
to the Mixer, which is
part of the control plane.

00:02:52.875 --> 00:02:54.250
And the Mixer then
can forward it

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:57.280
on to wherever you want that
telemetry information to go,

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:03.970
whether that's Stackdriver on
GCP, or Prometheus, or Datadog,

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:07.420
or wherever you want your
logging information to go,

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:10.680
we can do that
through the Mixer.

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:12.660
We provide operational agility.

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:15.720
So that means that you
can do canary rollouts.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:18.180
You can do blue/green rollouts.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:20.910
You can inject faults
into the network

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:24.000
if you want to test the
resiliency of your services.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.680
You can set quotas.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:29.190
If you've ever had a
service accidentally

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:31.350
do a DoS attack on one
of your other services,

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:35.930
then you might want to set
quotas on your services.

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:39.560
And then the third thing we can
do is policy-driven security.

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:45.860
So we can set encryption on
this-- on all the communication

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:47.130
going on on the network.

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:49.190
So we use mutual TLS.

00:03:49.190 --> 00:03:51.620
That means we set strong
identities for the service.

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:56.150
We know the identity of every
service running in the mesh.

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:58.780
And we know that service
A is talking to service B.

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:02.300
And so you see that service
B is talking to service C.

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:05.265
And you may think service
A is talking to service C.

00:04:05.265 --> 00:04:06.890
But again, by looking
at the telemetry,

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:10.290
you could see, maybe that's
not happening for some reason.

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:12.800
So we can do all
three of these things.

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.710
And these are very important
value props of Istio.

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:22.780
OK, as I mentioned, most
of the documentation,

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:26.560
most of the things you see about
Istio talk about a cluster.

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:29.680
And our control plane
runs in Kubernetes.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:32.720
So I've got Kubernetes
shown in the middle here.

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:36.610
But as you probably know, most
of the Enterprise workloads

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:38.140
actually still run on VMs.

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:41.060
And there are lots of
good reasons for that.

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:42.560
The first one is Datastores.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:45.280
So when I talk to customers,
probably the first thing

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:47.290
that comes up is Datastores.

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:50.680
They're running their MySQL,
their Postgres, their Oracle

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:54.610
database, their Cassandra,
their Couchbase, whatever it is.

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:57.150
And there is a whole
laundry list of them.

00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:00.430
Many, many Datastores
run on VMs today.

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:03.130
And many Enterprises
have no intention

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:04.800
of moving those
in the near-term.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:06.550
Some people are moving
them to Kubernetes.

00:05:06.550 --> 00:05:10.420
And Kubernetes is much
better now with those.

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:12.490
But a lot of them
are still on VMs

00:05:12.490 --> 00:05:14.978
and will be for quite a while.

00:05:14.978 --> 00:05:16.270
Then there's packaged software.

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:17.350
It's a black box, right?

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:19.240
You get it from some
vendor, whether it's

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:24.730
a CRM application, an HR
application, whatever it is.

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:29.230
The vendor says, this needs to
run on this release of Linux.

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:33.190
It needs X amount of
RAM, X amount of CPU.

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:35.360
That's where you'll run
it if you want support.

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:38.000
So as a company, you really
don't have any choice.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:40.470
That's going to run on a VM.

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:42.400
There is existing software.

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:44.950
Enterprises have
written lots of software

00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:48.340
on VMs in the last year, in the
last five years, the last 10

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:49.000
years.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:50.260
Those services are running.

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:52.240
They will be for a long time.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:55.720
And new applications are being
written on VMs all the time.

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:57.400
In most of your
Enterprises, you guys

00:05:57.400 --> 00:05:59.440
are still writing
software on VMs

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:01.550
and rolling out
new applications.

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:04.720
So we really want to bring all
these things into the Istio

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:08.860
mesh and provide all those
value props to you on these VMs.

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:13.740
OK.

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:15.970
So that's kind of the setup
and the level setting.

00:06:15.970 --> 00:06:18.540
So the rest of this
is really going to be,

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:19.630
so how do we do that?

00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:21.885
How do we bring VMs
into this environment?

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:27.670
So this diagram is a
picture of a Kubernetes

00:06:27.670 --> 00:06:29.380
cluster with Istio.

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:32.380
It's a simplified
representation.

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:36.290
I just show two services on
here, service 1 and service 2.

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:39.610
Now each one of those services
is some number of pods.

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:45.250
Let's say service 1 is three
pods, and service 2 is 10 pods.

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:48.910
Because we have Istio running
on here, those services,

00:06:48.910 --> 00:06:51.590
those pods are running
Istio sidecars as well.

00:06:51.590 --> 00:06:53.830
So imagine service
1 and service 2

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:56.650
are broken out into
those pods and they're

00:06:56.650 --> 00:06:59.590
running the Istio sidecars.

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:01.420
We have the control
plane components here

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:05.470
on the right, Mixer,
Pilot, and Citadel.

00:07:05.470 --> 00:07:09.310
Pilot is pushing out the policy
to all the Istio sidecars.

00:07:09.310 --> 00:07:11.530
It's the one that tells
it how to direct traffic

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:14.650
and all those kind of things,
what the security policy should

00:07:14.650 --> 00:07:15.730
be.

00:07:15.730 --> 00:07:17.380
Mixer is getting
all the requests

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:20.710
from the Istio sidecars
and the proxies.

00:07:20.710 --> 00:07:22.660
It's getting all that
telemetry information

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:24.670
and then forwarding on to--

00:07:24.670 --> 00:07:26.440
in what Jianfei will
show you in a minute

00:07:26.440 --> 00:07:29.030
is Prometheus in our case.

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:32.590
And then Citadel is the CA,
the Certification Authority.

00:07:32.590 --> 00:07:34.240
So it's providing
the certificates

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:38.560
for the strong authentication
between services.

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:41.950
Over on the left are
the ingress and egress

00:07:41.950 --> 00:07:43.970
gateways into and
out of the cluster.

00:07:43.970 --> 00:07:47.320
So this is what a standard
Kubernetes cluster looks

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:49.310
like with Istio installed.

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:51.820
And again, for those of you
that have been to other talks,

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:54.790
this is kind of where
this story stops.

00:07:54.790 --> 00:08:00.460
But what we want to do is
bring in VMs into this story.

00:08:00.460 --> 00:08:04.630
So again, two more simple
services on here, service 3

00:08:04.630 --> 00:08:06.290
and service 4.

00:08:06.290 --> 00:08:08.050
So how do we add
VMs to this world?

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:13.930
Well, there is some
setup we have to do.

00:08:13.930 --> 00:08:16.600
And we obviously have to
install some software.

00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:19.888
We're going to put the
Envoy proxy on to these VMs.

00:08:19.888 --> 00:08:22.180
I don't actually show it,
but there's another component

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:23.490
that goes on the VMs as well.

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:24.550
It's the node agent.

00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:26.770
And that's the thing
that talks to Citadel

00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:31.070
and is doing the certificate
renewal and so forth.

00:08:31.070 --> 00:08:33.549
So we have the Envoy proxy,
the node agent that we

00:08:33.549 --> 00:08:35.890
get installed on the VMs.

00:08:35.890 --> 00:08:37.390
And then we have
to tell the cluster

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:39.730
how to communicate with
the VMs and the VMs

00:08:39.730 --> 00:08:42.020
how to communicate
with the cluster.

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:44.710
So one of the important
things in that scenario

00:08:44.710 --> 00:08:46.160
is this service entry.

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:48.550
So you see these
little boxes here

00:08:48.550 --> 00:08:50.710
by the arrows that
go to the VMs?

00:08:50.710 --> 00:08:52.650
Those are service entries.

00:08:52.650 --> 00:08:55.390
A service entry
is a CRD that gets

00:08:55.390 --> 00:08:58.030
installed into the cluster
as part of the Istio install.

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:03.040
So let's take a look at that.

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:05.120
So I mentioned, it's a CRD.

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:08.480
It's a Configurable
Resource Definition.

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:10.700
This is the way you extend
Kubernetes and tell it

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:12.750
about new resources.

00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:14.420
This is a service entry.

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:15.440
It's what?

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:18.220
20 lines of YAML or so.

00:09:18.220 --> 00:09:20.360
And this is all we
need to do to supply

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:25.590
the cluster with information
about services on VMs.

00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:27.590
So some specific
information in here.

00:09:27.590 --> 00:09:30.990
The top box there
is the ServiceEntry.

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:35.400
That just says what type of
configurable resource this is.

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:37.930
So again, this is the
extension to Kubernetes.

00:09:37.930 --> 00:09:39.930
And then we're going to
provide some information

00:09:39.930 --> 00:09:41.520
about what the service is.

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:43.920
In this case, the
name of the service,

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:47.350
I just grabbed this YAML right
out the Istio documentation.

00:09:47.350 --> 00:09:49.240
So it's a simple example.

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:53.190
The name is the vmhttp.

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:56.610
We have to tell it what
the fully-qualified name

00:09:56.610 --> 00:09:58.050
of the host is.

00:09:58.050 --> 00:10:02.800
So here is vmhttp dot
SERVICE_NAMESPACE.

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:07.590
So again, we're dealing with
a Kubernetes control plane.

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:09.510
And Kubernetes has namespaces.

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:12.450
So we have to tell it where
this service is going to live,

00:10:12.450 --> 00:10:14.520
what namespace it's
going to live in.

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:17.070
You can create a new
namespace for your VMs.

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:19.310
You can put it in
existing namespaces.

00:10:19.310 --> 00:10:21.560
I think, actually the demo
you'll see in a few minutes

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:23.280
just uses the default namespace.

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:25.080
But it's totally up to you.

00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:28.710
You just have to put these
things in a namespace.

00:10:28.710 --> 00:10:30.900
And then some more
information about what

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:33.907
the address of the
actual host is,

00:10:33.907 --> 00:10:35.740
you need to know how
to communicate with it.

00:10:35.740 --> 00:10:39.810
So we have the GCE_IP address.

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:41.070
And then we have some labels.

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:43.140
So again, Kubernetes
uses labels.

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:46.920
And Istio uses labels for
things like canary rollouts

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:48.000
and directing traffic.

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:51.430
So we're going to add some
labels to this service as well.

00:10:51.430 --> 00:10:53.490
So that's all there
is to a service entry.

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:55.570
Again, fairly short.

00:10:55.570 --> 00:10:59.370
You just do a kube control
apply to enter this information

00:10:59.370 --> 00:11:00.750
into the Kubernetes cluster.

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:07.960
So let's pop back a level.

00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:09.777
We drove down into the CRD.

00:11:09.777 --> 00:11:11.360
But let's talk about
what you actually

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:15.470
have to do for the full setup
of adding a VM to your service

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:17.030
mesh.

00:11:17.030 --> 00:11:19.020
And this is right from
the Istio documentation.

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:21.060
This is just a summary of this.

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:23.490
So first, you have to
enable mesh expansion.

00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:28.550
This is just a Boolean
flag on the Istio install.

00:11:28.550 --> 00:11:30.650
By default, mesh
expansion is turned off.

00:11:30.650 --> 00:11:32.390
You just have to turn it on.

00:11:32.390 --> 00:11:35.160
It's a one-time operation
for the first VM you add.

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:37.620
And then if you add another
100 VMs, it doesn't matter.

00:11:37.620 --> 00:11:41.330
You don't have to
change that flag.

00:11:41.330 --> 00:11:43.520
You have to choose a
namespace for where these VMs

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:44.570
services are going to go.

00:11:44.570 --> 00:11:47.360
As I showed in that CRD, when
you register the service,

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:49.300
you have to say what
namespace that service

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:52.400
is associated with.

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:58.370
You have to add the IP of the
ingress gateway to the cluster

00:11:58.370 --> 00:11:59.600
to the VM.

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:01.820
So the VM is going to
communicate with the control

00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:02.320
plane.

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:05.120
It needs to know where to send
the telemetry information.

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:07.400
That information goes to Mixer.

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:09.860
And so we're going
to get to Mixer

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:12.780
through the ingress
gateway in the cluster.

00:12:12.780 --> 00:12:16.522
So you have to tell the VM
where that ingress gateway is.

00:12:16.522 --> 00:12:18.230
You're obviously going
to have to install

00:12:18.230 --> 00:12:21.740
the software on the VM, the
proxy, and the node agent.

00:12:21.740 --> 00:12:25.910
You can do that just
through the package install.

00:12:25.910 --> 00:12:31.850
And then step 6 and 7, these
are either/or or maybe both.

00:12:31.850 --> 00:12:35.270
Step 6 is if you
have a VM that's

00:12:35.270 --> 00:12:37.630
talking to services
inside the cluster--

00:12:37.630 --> 00:12:40.580
so the communication
is going from the VM

00:12:40.580 --> 00:12:42.650
to the service in the cluster--

00:12:42.650 --> 00:12:45.470
then you have to tell the
VM where that service is.

00:12:45.470 --> 00:12:48.290
So you just add its IP
address and information

00:12:48.290 --> 00:12:53.780
to [INAUDIBLE] hosts in the
sample we're going to show you.

00:12:53.780 --> 00:12:56.690
The other cases where you've got
the cluster talking to the VM,

00:12:56.690 --> 00:12:59.335
this is a very common case,
this is that Datastore case

00:12:59.335 --> 00:13:00.710
that I talked to
customers about.

00:13:00.710 --> 00:13:04.040
So they've got, say, a Postgres
database running on a VM

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:06.420
that they need to
access from the cluster.

00:13:06.420 --> 00:13:08.550
So now you have to tell
the cluster services

00:13:08.550 --> 00:13:10.280
how to get to the VM.

00:13:10.280 --> 00:13:12.230
You just create
that service entry,

00:13:12.230 --> 00:13:15.710
that YAML file I talked
about, do a kube control apply

00:13:15.710 --> 00:13:16.910
on that file.

00:13:16.910 --> 00:13:18.440
And then you have
to add the address

00:13:18.440 --> 00:13:20.992
to the DNS in the cluster.

00:13:20.992 --> 00:13:22.200
So that's all there is to it.

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:23.300
It's fairly simple.

00:13:23.300 --> 00:13:26.580
And actually, Jianfei
is going to show you

00:13:26.580 --> 00:13:27.970
exactly how to do that.

00:13:27.970 --> 00:13:31.260
So we're going to go
through two demos.

00:13:31.260 --> 00:13:32.970
The first one is the setup.

00:13:32.970 --> 00:13:34.978
The second one is
the Hipster app.

00:13:34.978 --> 00:13:37.020
And again, if you've been
to other presentations,

00:13:37.020 --> 00:13:39.240
