This should be Hipster
product catalog.

00:16:56.590 --> 00:16:57.340
All right.

00:16:57.340 --> 00:17:00.700
And by the way, for
today's demos purpose,

00:17:00.700 --> 00:17:03.970
I prepare a bare script to
automate a lot of steps,

00:17:03.970 --> 00:17:06.280
but essentially, that
it's all the same as a

00:17:06.280 --> 00:17:09.200
what you can find on
istio.io web page.

00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:11.480
Let's take a look
how that very quick,

00:17:11.480 --> 00:17:13.640
that batch automation
looks like.

00:17:13.640 --> 00:17:18.609
So pretty much that you can see
we need to create the cluster

00:17:18.609 --> 00:17:22.230
and create the GC VM,
download the Istio,

00:17:22.230 --> 00:17:23.500
and then install Istio.

00:17:23.500 --> 00:17:25.780
So there's nothing
special over there.

00:17:25.780 --> 00:17:32.440
That's just go back to the
demo and use the automation.

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:33.080
Cool.

00:17:33.080 --> 00:17:35.760
So first of the first,
you need to install Istio

00:17:35.760 --> 00:17:37.250
with mesh expansion enabled.

00:17:41.840 --> 00:17:43.770
We'll run this setup.

00:17:43.770 --> 00:17:50.010
And this will try to download
Istio and use your GKE cluster,

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:53.130
install the Istio with
its special [INAUDIBLE],,

00:17:53.130 --> 00:17:55.290
enable the mesh expansion.

00:17:55.290 --> 00:18:00.090
As Chris just mentioned that
the Istio mesh expansion to run

00:18:00.090 --> 00:18:04.710
the VM, you essentially need
to expose the Istio control

00:18:04.710 --> 00:18:10.380
plane to the VM workloads such
that as the Envoy on the VM

00:18:10.380 --> 00:18:13.880
know how to communicate with the
Istio control plane like pilot

00:18:13.880 --> 00:18:16.500
to get the Envoy
XDS configuration.

00:18:16.500 --> 00:18:19.050
Istio knows the agent
then can also reach out

00:18:19.050 --> 00:18:21.510
to the CA, Citadel
in the cluster

00:18:21.510 --> 00:18:24.240
to get key and serve refreshed.

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:27.300
And also for the Mixer, you need
to report the telemetric data

00:18:27.300 --> 00:18:31.420
back to Prometheus.

00:18:31.420 --> 00:18:31.920
Great.

00:18:31.920 --> 00:18:34.740
So we just finished
the installation.

00:18:34.740 --> 00:18:40.830
And now we need to do another
thing to set up the GC VM.

00:18:46.860 --> 00:18:50.390
We will run another
command for that purpose.

00:18:50.390 --> 00:18:54.270
As you can see, other than
command, we have a 3550 port.

00:18:54.270 --> 00:18:57.170
That is the port value the
VM applications, the product

00:18:57.170 --> 00:19:02.660
catalog service, specifically,
that will listen on 3550.

00:19:02.660 --> 00:19:05.150
And let's go ahead and see
what we are doing there.

00:19:05.150 --> 00:19:09.960
The first thing is to get some
configuration from the cluster,

00:19:09.960 --> 00:19:13.670
determine the IP range of
the service in the cluster.

00:19:13.670 --> 00:19:18.230
And the second thing is to
get the key insert for this VM

00:19:18.230 --> 00:19:19.280
workload.

00:19:19.280 --> 00:19:21.650
Remember, Chris just
mentioned that you

00:19:21.650 --> 00:19:26.270
need to choose a namespace
and a service account

00:19:26.270 --> 00:19:29.180
to represent the
workload on the VM

00:19:29.180 --> 00:19:31.620
as that VM workload identity.

00:19:31.620 --> 00:19:33.950
So for our demo's
purpose, we just

00:19:33.950 --> 00:19:36.110
choose the default and
the default service

00:19:36.110 --> 00:19:38.468
account, default namespace.

00:19:38.468 --> 00:19:40.010
But you can choose
whatever you like.

00:19:43.160 --> 00:19:43.710
Cool.

00:19:43.710 --> 00:19:45.300
So this two kind
of configuration

00:19:45.300 --> 00:19:47.400
now is dumped into
your workstation.

00:19:47.400 --> 00:19:49.620
And then you need
to copy it over

00:19:49.620 --> 00:19:53.630
to your GC VM, which is
essentially just a GCloud

00:19:53.630 --> 00:19:55.940
compute SEP command line.

00:19:55.940 --> 00:19:59.450
You can see that a
bunch of configuration

00:19:59.450 --> 00:20:03.850
has been copied to
the GC instance.

00:20:03.850 --> 00:20:06.850
Once we have all those
configuration being copied

00:20:06.850 --> 00:20:11.920
over, now is the time to run
some automation on the GC

00:20:11.920 --> 00:20:12.770
instance.

00:20:12.770 --> 00:20:15.610
So this part of the script
now will be running inside

00:20:15.610 --> 00:20:17.200
of that VM instance.

00:20:20.770 --> 00:20:27.030
So as you can see that we
expose the-- put the gateway IP,

00:20:27.030 --> 00:20:30.760
a public IP, onto
the /etc/hosts.

00:20:30.760 --> 00:20:34.530
So the Envoy can use
a name, Istio dash

00:20:34.530 --> 00:20:38.130
pilot to resolve the address
of the Istio control plane

00:20:38.130 --> 00:20:38.880
through gateway.

00:20:41.730 --> 00:20:45.210
And this part is just trying
to download the Istio Debian

00:20:45.210 --> 00:20:51.840
package that will be installed
on the VM operating system.

00:20:51.840 --> 00:20:56.720
And we also need to copy the
key, cert to the designated

00:20:56.720 --> 00:21:03.810
file system pass for the
Envoy to talking [INAUDIBLE]..

00:21:03.810 --> 00:21:04.910
All right.

00:21:04.910 --> 00:21:09.620
And the very last step,
after you set up everything,

00:21:09.620 --> 00:21:11.720
you can now start
the Istio component

00:21:11.720 --> 00:21:14.630
on the VM which
specifically is just

00:21:14.630 --> 00:21:19.400
Istio Envoy used for intercept
of the traffic and Istio node

00:21:19.400 --> 00:21:22.130
agent that is responsible
for refreshing

00:21:22.130 --> 00:21:24.800
the key and cert
before it expires.

00:21:27.460 --> 00:21:27.960
That's it.

00:21:27.960 --> 00:21:29.770
Let's see second step.

00:21:29.770 --> 00:21:34.320
So now you have both Istio
installed in your Kubernetes

00:21:34.320 --> 00:21:37.080
cluster and you
have the VM that is

00:21:37.080 --> 00:21:39.120
ready to run some applications.

00:21:39.120 --> 00:21:41.610
Actually, if you
just type some--

00:21:41.610 --> 00:21:44.280
run your applications
on that VM,

00:21:44.280 --> 00:21:46.960
the Envoy is ready to
intercept the traffic.

00:21:46.960 --> 00:21:49.810
And you can find
the other service.

00:21:49.810 --> 00:21:51.440
It's in the Kubernetes cluster.

00:21:51.440 --> 00:21:53.320
And we are ready to go.

00:21:53.320 --> 00:21:56.880
However, there is one
thing that is missing,

00:21:56.880 --> 00:21:59.250
that is the service discovery.

00:21:59.250 --> 00:22:03.650
As Chris just mentioned,
because for the applications

00:22:03.650 --> 00:22:05.790
that inside of the
Kubernetes cluster,

00:22:05.790 --> 00:22:09.420
they don't know there is some
service running on this VM.

00:22:09.420 --> 00:22:12.030
We need kind of service
discovery mechanism

00:22:12.030 --> 00:22:15.060
to model these VM services.

00:22:15.060 --> 00:22:18.480
The Istio, to handle
this as we know--

00:22:18.480 --> 00:22:21.150
just learned-- it's
using a service entry.

00:22:21.150 --> 00:22:25.560
So we basically need to write
some CRD of the service entry

00:22:25.560 --> 00:22:29.629
to map a VM service to
a list of the endpoints.

00:22:34.620 --> 00:22:38.660
So here we choose the name of
the services, product catalog

00:22:38.660 --> 00:22:39.560
services.

00:22:39.560 --> 00:22:43.280
And the port value is 3550,
match what we just used.

00:22:43.280 --> 00:22:45.160
And the protocol is the grpc.

00:22:45.160 --> 00:22:50.930
That is the three
arguments for this command.

00:22:50.930 --> 00:22:51.430
Great.

00:22:51.430 --> 00:22:53.680
We just did that.

00:22:53.680 --> 00:22:56.720
So this essentially
do two things.

00:22:56.720 --> 00:23:02.710
First is to create a service
entry to this IP of the VMs.

00:23:02.710 --> 00:23:04.780
And the second thing is
to create a Kubernetes

00:23:04.780 --> 00:23:06.880
service, selector service.

00:23:06.880 --> 00:23:08.920
The purpose of that
is that when you

00:23:08.920 --> 00:23:11.170
create a service in the
Kubernetes cluster, it will--

00:23:11.170 --> 00:23:15.580
the Kubernetes is able to
return a corresponding DNS

00:23:15.580 --> 00:23:20.480
record for the workload in the
Kubernetes cluster to resolve.

00:23:20.480 --> 00:23:23.800
So the Kubernetes
applications can

00:23:23.800 --> 00:23:28.570
use the names instead of IPs.

00:23:28.570 --> 00:23:32.520
And so let's take a look what's
the service entry we just

00:23:32.520 --> 00:23:33.570
generated looks like.

00:23:39.558 --> 00:23:40.556
All right.

00:23:49.040 --> 00:23:49.570
Sorry.

00:23:49.570 --> 00:23:51.050
Need to upload the YAML file.

00:23:53.920 --> 00:23:54.670
All right.

00:23:54.670 --> 00:23:57.280
So as you can see--

00:23:57.280 --> 00:24:02.872
probably need to
scroll up a little bit.

00:24:02.872 --> 00:24:09.250
[INAUDIBLE] So this is
the service entries CRD.

00:24:09.250 --> 00:24:13.270
And the name is product
catalog service dashboard VM.

00:24:13.270 --> 00:24:19.270
We use that denote it's a
service running on the VM.

00:24:19.270 --> 00:24:22.400
And you can see
in the spec ports,

00:24:22.400 --> 00:24:24.430
the first is the endpoints.

00:24:24.430 --> 00:24:27.380
The endpoints list the
list of the endpoints

00:24:27.380 --> 00:24:31.230
that is responsible for
backing up this service.

00:24:31.230 --> 00:24:35.380
In each endpoint, you can
specify the IP address,

00:24:35.380 --> 00:24:38.070
which is, in our case,
is the VM IP address

00:24:38.070 --> 00:24:41.310
to run the Hipster
product catalog service.

00:24:41.310 --> 00:24:43.770
And you can also put
the labels over there.

00:24:43.770 --> 00:24:46.240
Labels, you are familiar
with Kubernetes,

00:24:46.240 --> 00:24:51.240
is you can put the
denotation on the deployment.

00:24:51.240 --> 00:24:55.010
And you can use labels to select
Manage your workload later.

00:24:55.010 --> 00:24:59.010
And as you can see, here
we put the register column

00:24:59.010 --> 00:25:01.080
rawvm labels here.

00:25:01.080 --> 00:25:06.490
And we are take advantage of
that in our follow-up demo.

00:25:06.490 --> 00:25:08.770
And, of course, we need
to specify the host,

00:25:08.770 --> 00:25:10.250
the name of the service.

00:25:10.250 --> 00:25:12.280
And the port is 3550.

00:25:12.280 --> 00:25:13.830
Protocol is grpc.

00:25:13.830 --> 00:25:16.510
And that's it.

00:25:16.510 --> 00:25:17.040
Cool.

00:25:17.040 --> 00:25:20.790
So actually, now that is
pretty much everything

00:25:20.790 --> 00:25:25.140
you need to do for the
VM setup mesh dimension.

00:25:25.140 --> 00:25:29.670
And, of course, you need to
run your applications product

00:25:29.670 --> 00:25:31.410
catalog service on that VM.

00:25:31.410 --> 00:25:35.280
But we are going to skip that
part and switch to a cluster

00:25:35.280 --> 00:25:36.960
that we already
finished that part.

00:25:48.990 --> 00:25:51.790
So for these Hipster
app, everything

00:25:51.790 --> 00:25:54.330
is passed through
a frontend ingress.

00:25:54.330 --> 00:25:57.730
And here is the public IP
of the frontend ingress.

00:26:01.230 --> 00:26:04.960
Let's send some
requests to this app

00:26:04.960 --> 00:26:08.400
to see what is going on there.

00:26:08.400 --> 00:26:12.230
So you keep hit the Refresh
button couple of times.

00:26:12.230 --> 00:26:19.630
And, oh, OK, so here we go.

00:26:19.630 --> 00:26:23.490
As we can see, sometimes it will
show a list of the products.

00:26:23.490 --> 00:26:26.580
Sometimes, it only
shows one product.

00:26:26.580 --> 00:26:28.200
Why that is the case?

00:26:28.200 --> 00:26:30.960
Because we actually
runs a product catalog

00:26:30.960 --> 00:26:34.950
service on both Kubernetes
cluster and on the GC VM.

00:26:34.950 --> 00:26:37.200
In some sense,
this single service

00:26:37.200 --> 00:26:40.020
is spinning all across
different platforms,

00:26:40.020 --> 00:26:43.190
whether it's from the VM or
it's on Kubernetes cluster.

00:26:43.190 --> 00:26:46.900
From a mesh point of
view, it doesn't matter.

00:26:46.900 --> 00:26:52.590
And so to create a visual
very distinguishable effect,

00:26:52.590 --> 00:26:56.130
we curated the items to
only retain one product

00:26:56.130 --> 00:26:58.080
item on the VM version.

00:26:58.080 --> 00:27:02.100
And then for the Kubernetes one,
we have more products for you

00:27:02.100 --> 00:27:05.470
to buy from.

00:27:05.470 --> 00:27:08.450
And if you're not
by the default case,

00:27:08.450 --> 00:27:11.710
then you are now using any
advanced virtual service

00:27:11.710 --> 00:27:13.750
kind of routing configuration.

00:27:13.750 --> 00:27:18.400
It's randomly select either
one of the endpoint, either VM

00:27:18.400 --> 00:27:21.550
endpoint or the pod in
the Kubernetes cluster.

00:27:21.550 --> 00:27:24.160
So it's 50 by 50 chance.

00:27:24.160 --> 00:27:30.990
And we will see that
through the dashboard later.

00:27:30.990 --> 00:27:31.500
Cool.

00:27:31.500 --> 00:27:36.980
So now let's send some traffic
to simulate the real customer

00:27:36.980 --> 00:27:37.580
traffic.

00:27:37.580 --> 00:27:40.610
We have a Python script
to generate some traffic

00:27:40.610 --> 00:27:41.840
against that frontend.

00:27:45.760 --> 00:27:47.260
So let's stop.

00:27:51.550 --> 00:27:53.440
Let me explain a
little bit of what

00:27:53.440 --> 00:27:55.030
this Python script is doing.

00:27:55.030 --> 00:27:58.330
So this Python script is sending
some requests to the front end

00:27:58.330 --> 00:28:00.220
and parse the HTML.

00:28:00.220 --> 00:28:02.590
And if it is only
return one items,

00:28:02.590 --> 00:28:04.450
then we know it is from the VM.

00:28:04.450 --> 00:28:07.120
And if it is returns
more items, then it

00:28:07.120 --> 00:28:09.550
is from Kubernetes cluster.

00:28:09.550 --> 00:28:13.840
So in such a way that we can
count the accumulated requests

00:28:13.840 --> 00:28:16.120
that is either handled
by the Kubernetes cluster

00:28:16.120 --> 00:28:20.110
or they handled by
the VM workload.

00:28:20.110 --> 00:28:23.720
And so as you can see,
this is roughly 50 and 50.

00:28:23.720 --> 00:28:25.060
There's just pure random.

00:28:29.270 --> 00:28:32.900
We're going to just run
these scripts continuously

00:28:32.900 --> 00:28:35.130
to generate some traffic.

00:28:35.130 --> 00:28:40.950
Now as you know, of the
value proposition for Istio

00:28:40.950 --> 00:28:45.140
is to have the out of
box telemetry feature.

00:28:45.140 --> 00:28:51.870
And let me show you on
the Grafana dashboard.

00:28:51.870 --> 00:28:53.390
So here is the Grafana.

00:28:53.390 --> 00:28:55.580
Probably, I need to
zoom in a little bit.

00:28:58.150 --> 00:29:01.230
So we chose the namespace
default, which--

00:29:01.230 --> 00:29:04.040
and the workload,
we can see here,

00:29:04.040 --> 00:29:06.310
there is both
productcatalogservice

00:29:06.310 --> 00:29:10.180
and product catalog
dash rawvm and there

00:29:10.180 --> 00:29:12.300
is productcatalogservice.

00:29:12.300 --> 00:29:14.710
The upper part of
this part result

00:29:14.710 --> 00:29:18.370
is the Kubernetes version
that is using the Kubernetes

00:29:18.370 --> 00:29:22.810
deployment name to
denote the workload name.

00:29:22.810 --> 00:29:25.000
And the second part
is matches what

00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:27.130
we used in the service entry.

00:29:27.130 --> 00:29:32.710
And we used this
for the VM workload.

00:29:32.710 --> 00:29:34.570
Less refresh it a little bit.

00:29:39.480 --> 00:29:41.150
As you can see that--

00:29:41.150 --> 00:29:45.510
so it has some qps 2 qps.

00:29:45.510 --> 00:29:50.460
And this is the response
by response code, it 200.

00:29:50.460 --> 00:29:52.910
And it works great.

00:29:52.910 --> 00:29:55.890
Let's do another
tab, side by side,

00:29:55.890 --> 00:29:58.660
to see the traffic that is
running on the Kubernetes

00:29:58.660 --> 00:29:59.160
cluster.

00:30:02.270 --> 00:30:03.780
And it's 3 qps.

00:30:03.780 --> 00:30:09.330
So it's no that's accurate,
but it is roughly at like 50%

00:30:09.330 --> 00:30:11.440
by 50%.

00:30:11.440 --> 00:30:15.770
So you get the telemetry
feature from the Istio.

00:30:15.770 --> 00:30:18.310
Now let's say you want
to protect your traffic.

00:30:18.310 --> 00:30:22.390
You want to enable the mutual
TLS handled by the Envoy.

00:30:22.390 --> 00:30:24.740
You don't need to do anything
on your applications.

00:30:24.740 --> 00:30:30.260
So for that, we are just write
some authentication policy.

00:30:30.260 --> 00:30:36.190
So as some of you
familiar with the Istio,

00:30:36.190 --> 00:30:41.560
we use authentication policy
to configure the server side

00:30:41.560 --> 00:30:43.470
security requirement.

00:30:43.470 --> 00:30:47.680
Basically, we say, for this
service, for these part,

00:30:47.680 --> 00:30:50.250
we require you to either--

00:30:50.250 --> 00:30:52.320
the client needs to
talk in mutual TLS

00:30:52.320 --> 00:30:54.720
or you need to give
me a [INAUDIBLE]..

00:30:54.720 --> 00:30:57.940
For our purpose, we are
just using mutual TLS

00:30:57.940 --> 00:31:01.420
And we have some CRD here.

00:31:05.792 --> 00:31:07.500
Let's take a look at
how that looks like.

00:31:07.500 --> 00:31:10.500
The policy specified the
productcatalogservice.

00:31:10.500 --> 00:31:14.960
And the mutual TLS we specify
the mode as the strict.

00:31:14.960 --> 00:31:19.080
Also, that is for server-side
requirements that clients,

00:31:19.080 --> 00:31:22.800
you please talk in mutual
TLS, otherwise I will reject.

00:31:22.800 --> 00:31:27.030
And for the client side,
that is the destination rule.

00:31:27.030 --> 00:31:30.600
Destination rule tells how
the client Envoy, what kind

00:31:30.600 --> 00:31:35.220
of mutual TLS settings
you need to use when

00:31:35.220 --> 00:31:37.440
talk to the specific server.

00:31:37.440 --> 00:31:39.870
So we specify ISTIO_MUTUAL.

00:31:39.870 --> 00:31:42.420
And here so that client
and server, both we

00:31:42.420 --> 00:31:45.200
are use expected results.

00:31:53.300 --> 00:31:55.820
So let's apply this YAML file.

00:31:55.820 --> 00:31:59.110
And as you can see, both
policy and the destination rule

00:31:59.110 --> 00:32:00.550
now has been configured.

00:32:00.550 --> 00:32:02.050
We are expecting the traffic.

00:32:02.050 --> 00:32:05.950
We are shifting from the
plain text to the mutual TLS

00:32:05.950 --> 00:32:08.672
Let's verify that on
our Grafana dashboard.

00:32:13.400 --> 00:32:17.900
Just it take a couple of seconds
for the CRD from the API server

00:32:17.900 --> 00:32:20.180
to go to the pilot and
then goes to Envoy.

00:32:24.120 --> 00:32:24.930
Let me refresh it.

00:32:24.930 --> 00:32:27.460
Oh, actually, we can see that.

00:32:27.460 --> 00:32:35.700
Now the traffic has been swapped
from the yellow to the green.

00:32:35.700 --> 00:32:39.280
From the yellow, you can
see that it's nothing there.

00:32:39.280 --> 00:32:44.910
But if you hand over
to the green line,

00:32:44.910 --> 00:32:50.370
you can see there is a
column, mutual TLS which

00:32:50.370 --> 00:32:54.350
means that your traffic now
is encrypted and protected.

00:32:54.350 --> 00:32:58.040
And these behaviors, same
consistently regardless

00:32:58.040 --> 00:32:59.920
whether you run your VM--

00:32:59.920 --> 00:33:02.360
the application's on the VM
or your Kubernetes cluster.

00:33:02.360 --> 00:33:06.170
You can see both of them get
swapped from the plain text

00:33:06.170 --> 00:33:06.950
mutual TLS.

00:33:09.870 --> 00:33:10.370
Awesome.

00:33:10.370 --> 00:33:13.190
So we finish the security part.

00:33:13.190 --> 00:33:17.480
Now let's talking about
the networking management.

00:33:17.480 --> 00:33:19.700
So you run your
applications on the VM.

00:33:19.700 --> 00:33:22.730
And you run something on
the Kubernetes cluster.

00:33:22.730 --> 00:33:25.430
Imagine that you want to
migrate something from the VM

00:33:25.430 --> 00:33:26.960
to the Kubernetes cluster.

00:33:26.960 --> 00:33:30.710
And you want to control
this process and [INAUDIBLE]

00:33:30.710 --> 00:33:33.560
the migration with competence.

00:33:33.560 --> 00:33:35.490
Istio supporters
that while advanced

00:33:35.490 --> 00:33:38.030
L7 and routing using
virtual service.

00:33:38.030 --> 00:33:39.920
And you just write
some virtual service

00:33:39.920 --> 00:33:43.880
CRDs to configure how much
percentage of the traffic

00:33:43.880 --> 00:33:45.170
you want to send to the--

00:33:45.170 --> 00:33:48.410
and one of the subset which
is Kubernetes cluster,

00:33:48.410 --> 00:33:51.320
how much you want to
send to the VM subset.

00:33:55.070 --> 00:33:58.340
We also have-- already
have some CRD for that.

00:34:03.690 --> 00:34:04.750
Here we go.

00:34:04.750 --> 00:34:07.710
So the first part, again,
is the destination rule.

00:34:07.710 --> 00:34:11.739
And in the destination rule,
other than the TLS settings,

00:34:11.739 --> 00:34:13.679
we also specify two subsets.

00:34:13.679 --> 00:34:15.830
The first one is Kubernetes one.

00:34:15.830 --> 00:34:20.150
If we use the labels,
labels that's basically

00:34:20.150 --> 00:34:23.500
for the workload, that has app
column productcatalogservices

00:34:23.500 --> 00:34:25.280
labels that we are--

00:34:25.280 --> 00:34:28.330
make that as part of
the Kubernetes subset.

00:34:28.330 --> 00:34:33.219
And for the endpoint that
has the registry [INAUDIBLE]

00:34:33.219 --> 00:34:38.440
labels, that we are think
that is a rawvm, the vm

00:34:38.440 --> 00:34:40.159
part of the subset.

00:34:40.159 --> 00:34:43.929
So remember that
the registry column

00:34:43.929 --> 00:34:46.659
rawvm, these labels, that
is exactly what you just

00:34:46.659 --> 00:34:48.260
put it in the service entry.

00:34:48.260 --> 00:34:50.260
Basically, in the
Kubernetes, you

00:34:50.260 --> 00:34:52.630
put labels in your deployment.

00:34:52.630 --> 00:34:55.480
And in the service entry, we
have the same functionality

00:34:55.480 --> 00:35:02.080
for you to attach labels and
use that to define the subset.

00:35:02.080 --> 00:35:05.500
Once you define the
subset of service,

00:35:05.500 --> 00:35:08.980
you can then use the virtual
service for the routing.

00:35:08.980 --> 00:35:13.930
As you can see that for the
Kubernetes subset, we use 80%.

00:35:13.930 --> 00:35:16.930
And for the VM part, we use 20%.

00:35:16.930 --> 00:35:22.510
So we are expecting after
apply these two CRDs, the more

00:35:22.510 --> 00:35:28.270
traffic from 50 will jump to the
80 for the Kubernetes traffic.

00:35:28.270 --> 00:35:31.090
And the VM, we get less traffic.

00:35:45.050 --> 00:35:47.440
So the configuration
has been applied.

00:35:50.030 --> 00:35:54.050
Now what we are expecting
is a dropping line

00:35:54.050 --> 00:35:56.450
on the VM Grafana dashboard.

00:35:56.450 --> 00:36:02.895
And we will a bump up in
the Kubernetes workload.

00:36:02.895 --> 00:36:04.520
So this might take
a couple of seconds.

00:36:08.170 --> 00:36:10.190
But here we go.

00:36:10.190 --> 00:36:15.090
So you can see the line is
dropping down from around 2 qps

00:36:15.090 --> 00:36:17.140
to 1 qps.

00:36:17.140 --> 00:36:20.490
And on the other hand,
the Kubernetes workload

00:36:20.490 --> 00:36:28.480
is significantly ramp up
because more traffic is routed

00:36:28.480 --> 00:36:30.350
to the Kubernetes cluster.

00:36:30.350 --> 00:36:38.010
So this seems all right.

00:36:38.010 --> 00:36:43.110
And as you have
more confidence, you

00:36:43.110 --> 00:36:45.640
can continue to use
the virtual service

00:36:45.640 --> 00:36:48.100
to route more traffic to
your Kubernetes version

00:36:48.100 --> 00:36:51.220
and finally get rid
of your VM workload.

00:36:51.220 --> 00:36:54.640
But that's our demo.

00:36:54.640 --> 00:37:02.800
And to summarize, the take away
that you have some VM workload.

00:37:02.800 --> 00:37:05.500
And you want to migrate
to Kubernetes cluster.

00:37:05.500 --> 00:37:08.710
You want to have
the service mesh

00:37:08.710 --> 00:37:10.990
across different environments.

00:37:10.990 --> 00:37:15.400
And you may even want to
migrate something from the VM

00:37:15.400 --> 00:37:16.790
to the Kubernetes cluster.

00:37:16.790 --> 00:37:21.070
And Istio can help you to finish
that journey with confidence

00:37:21.070 --> 00:37:26.030
by having telemetry
and showing you what

00:37:26.030 --> 00:37:28.390
is going on in that process.

